1. Arrays in Bash
= An Arrays  is a variable that can store multiple values.

# Syntax
array_name=("value1" "value2" "value3")


Example:
#!/bin/bash
servers=("server1" "server2" "server3")
echo ${servers[0]}


# Example Looping Through an Array
#!/bin/bash
services=("ngnix" "docker" "mysql")

for service in
"${services{@}}"
do
   echo "Checking
$services..."
done



2. Reading Files in Bash
=Bash can read files line by line using the while read loop.

Example File:
servers.txt
= server1
  server2
  server3

# Script
#!/bin/bash
while read server
do
 echo "Checking
$server..."
done < servers.txt


3. Case Statement
= The case statement is used when multiple conditions depend
on the same variable.

Example:

#!/bin/bash

echo "Enter Action:"
read action

case $action in
    start)
        echo "Starting Service..."
        ;;
    stop)
        echo "Stopping Service..."
        ;;
    restart)
        echo "Restarting Service..."
        ;;
    *)
        echo "Invalid Option"
        ;;
esac


4. Basic Error Handling
= Every Linux command return an Exit Status.

Exit Status
   0 = Sucess
 Non-zero = Error


Example:
#!/bin/bash
mkdir DemoFolder
if [ $? -eq 0 ]
then
echo "Folder Created
Successfully"
else
echo "Folder Already
Exists"
fi
